<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  ==================================================================================================
  security-service.slnx
  The per service solution for the Security service of the PowerFramework .NET 10 decomposition.
  ==================================================================================================

  WHY THIS FILE EXISTS AT ALL

    The mandated per service workflow is run from THIS directory, and every verb is invoked with no
    solution argument, no project argument and no path argument whatsoever:

        cd services/security-service
        dotnet restore
        dotnet build -c Release
        dotnet test (collecting XPlat Code Coverage)

    A bare invocation resolves its target by finding exactly one solution file in the current
    directory. This file is that solution file. Enumerating Security's two projects here is the
    entire mechanism by which Security restores, builds and tests standalone from a clean checkout,
    with no sibling service present, no shared solution and no orchestration running. That property
    is what makes this service independently deployable rather than a folder inside a monolith, and
    it is the whole reason a solution file exists per service.

  WHAT THIS SERVICE IS, SO THE TWO MEMBERS BELOW READ IN CONTEXT

      Port        5104.
      Transport   REST plus OpenAPI. Token issuance and key publication have to be plain HTTP so
                  that each consumer's stock JwtBearer handler fetches the JWKS document and the
                  OpenID discovery metadata with no bespoke code at all. Choosing gRPC here would
                  push hand written key retrieval into the other three services, a net increase in
                  hand written security code rather than a decrease.
      Role        SOLE JWT ISSUER for the whole system. Exactly one signing secret exists anywhere
                  in this decomposition and this service holds it. Gateway, DataServices and
                  Persistence hold verification material only and never mint a token.
      Replaces    the legacy framework's cryptographic library, whose single native class declares
                  65 public overloads: encoding conversion, random and GUID generation, RSA key
                  generation, unkeyed hashing and keyed HMAC, symmetric encryption and decryption,
                  and RSA signing plus verification. Every one is reimplemented on the Base Class
                  Library, so the signing primitives a token issuer needs are already present.

    NONE of that is configured here. A port is a runtime concern, a transport is an application
    concern, and a secret is an orchestration concern reaching the service through the options
    pattern. A solution declares membership and nothing else, so those facts appear above purely so
    a reader knows what the two projects below contain. They are specified in docs/ARCHITECTURE.md.

  THE FORMAT IS .slnx, NEVER .sln, AND NEVER ALONGSIDE A .slnf

    .NET 10 tooling emits the XML solution format. Measured on the pinned SDK 10.0.302 before this
    file was written rather than taken from documentation: `dotnet new sln` produces a bare Solution
    root element, and adding a project produces a Project child carrying a Path attribute whose
    value uses forward slash separators and is relative to the solution file's own directory. The
    body below follows that shape exactly. No .sln text format file exists anywhere in this
    repository, and the required name for this file carries the .slnx extension.

    Do not author a .slnf solution filter here or anywhere else. The planning record documents a
    legacy filter failing against a .slnx format solution with MSB4014 during restore, during build
    AND during test, and a filter would be pointless here regardless: this solution has two members
    and neither is one you would ever want filtered out. The per service .slnx design IS the
    resolution that a filter would have been reached for.

    A second solution file in this directory is worse than pointless, it is actively breaking. With
    more than one, the bare commands above stop resolving anything and fail with MSB1011, "specify
    which project or solution file to use because this folder contains more than one project or
    solution file". That was reproduced deliberately on SDK 10.0.302 to confirm the constraint is
    real rather than folklore, then reverted. Exactly one solution file belongs here, and it is this
    one.

    One more measured toolchain behaviour, recorded because the temptation is to correct it from
    here: a bare `dotnet test` builds Debug even immediately after `dotnet build -c Release`. The
    release configuration form passes the configuration to the test verb as well. That is documented
    in docs/BUILD.md and must not be worked around from inside a solution file, which has no
    configuration to express.

  RELATIONSHIP TO THE REPOSITORY ROOT SOLUTION

    The repository root PowerFramework.slnx enumerates all twenty projects in the refactor,
    including this service's two. The two solutions coexist by design and neither supersedes the
    other: the root one is the whole estate convenience view for a single pass build of everything,
    while this one is the independently buildable unit that CI and the container build exercise per
    service. Solution discovery inspects the current directory only and never walks up, so the root
    solution can neither be found by nor interfere with a bare command issued in this directory.

    Both files must always name the same two Security project paths. Renaming or relocating either
    project is therefore a change to both files and to the sibling Dockerfile, which copies and
    publishes these same paths from a repository root build context.

  EXACTLY TWO MEMBERS, AND EXACTLY THESE TWO

    PowerFramework.Security
      The application. Its own ProjectReference entries reach the published contracts project and
      the shared kernel, so the whole shared closure arrives transitively and is deliberately NOT
      listed here. Solution membership is not how a project expresses a dependency: MSBuild derives
      the build graph from project references, so those two shared projects restore and build from
      this solution without appearing in it, which was verified by executing the workflow above.
      Listing them would mean paths climbing out of this directory and a second inventory to keep in
      step with the root solution's, for no gain.

      One consequence of that choice was measured, and it is FIXED at the repository root rather than
      by changing this file. When a solution build reaches a project that is not itself a member,
      MSBuild's default is to unset the parent configuration so the referenced project builds in its
      own default. Observed on SDK 10.0.302 before the fix: `dotnet build -c Release` in this
      directory built both members below into bin/Release as intended, while the two shared libraries
      landed in bin/Debug, and the identical behaviour was confirmed against the other three per
      service solutions. That is a real defect and not a quirk to live with, because it makes a
      Release artifact consume assemblies compiled with Debug settings while the command still reports
      a clean Release build.

      The repository root Directory.Build.props now sets ShouldUnsetParentConfigurationAndPlatform to
      false, so this solution's configuration is passed through to the projects it references but does
      not list, and it carries an assertion target that fails the build (PFW0001) if any referenced
      output ever arrives from a different configuration again. Re-measured after the change: both
      shared projects land in bin/Release, with zero warnings. The repository root solution, which
      does list every project, is unaffected because the solution configuration still maps its members
      explicitly.

      Adding the shared projects to this file remains the wrong fix and must not be done: it would buy
      nothing that is not already guaranteed, at the price of the brittle out of directory paths
      described above, and it would leave this one service shaped differently from its three siblings.
      Overriding ShouldUnsetParentConfigurationAndPlatform back to true is equally wrong, and the
      assertion target exists precisely so that doing so fails loudly instead of silently.

    PowerFramework.Security.Tests
      The tests, load bearing rather than a courtesy. This project is the only producer of
      Security's coverage.cobertura.xml, and that report is the exact artifact the 80 percent line
      coverage per service gate is measured from. Leave it out of this solution and a bare test run
      in this directory discovers nothing, emits no report, and the gate cannot be evaluated at all,
      which is a failure that reads exactly like a pass.

    Both paths are relative to this file's own directory and neither rises above it, which is what
    keeps them valid when the image is built from a repository root context.

    No sibling service project appears here. Security serves Gateway, DataServices and Persistence
    over the network through the published contracts, never through a project reference, because one
    solution spanning several services is precisely the collapsed outcome this decomposition exists
    to avoid. It is a failure mode rather than a cautious default.

    No deferred capability project appears here either, in any form: no project, no solution folder,
    no placeholder and no commented out line reserving a slot. The four deferred capability areas
    are outside this phase entirely and are inventoried once, in docs/DEFERRED.md. Their only
    representation anywhere is four reserved routes in the Gateway application's routing metadata,
    and nothing compiles behind those.

  NOTHING IS CONFIGURED HERE, AND NOTHING IS SECRET HERE

    No MSBuild property, no package version, no target framework, no custom configuration and no
    custom platform appears in this file, and there are no project GUIDs or configuration mapping
    blocks: the XML format needs none of that legacy ceremony. Only the standard Debug and Release
    configurations are in play and nothing in this refactor requires another.

    Everything else arrives from the repository root by MSBuild directory walk up. global.json pins
    the SDK version with roll forward confined to the latest feature band - the number is deliberately
    not repeated here, for the reason the rest of this paragraph gives - Directory.Build.props sets
    net10.0, nullable reference types, implicit usings, warnings as errors and the analyzers and
    turns central package management on, and Directory.Packages.props holds every package version
    including the two mandatory security pins that clear the NuGet audit advisories. Restating any
    of it here would create a second source of truth able to drift this one service off a mandatory
    pin, the exact failure central package management exists to prevent. For the same reason this
    directory carries no per service Directory.Build.props, Directory.Packages.props, global.json,
    .editorconfig or .dockerignore.

    No credential, key, token or connection string appears here, and none ever may. This service is
    the sole holder of the system's one signing secret and that secret reaches it at run time from
    the orchestration secret layer through the options pattern only. Nothing of the sort belongs in
    a solution file, a project file, an application settings file or a container definition.

  LEGACY PROVENANCE: ONE IDEA BORROWED, ONE MECHANISM DELIBERATELY NOT TRANSLATED

    The conceptual ancestor of this file is the PowerBuilder workspace pfw.pbw, which does no more
    than enumerate its child targets by relative path and name a default among them. The borrowed
    idea is exactly that: one container enumerating buildable units and carrying no configuration.

    What is NOT translated is the library list. The primary target pfw.pbt declares a single ordered
    list of 32 libraries and its ORDER is load bearing there, because PowerBuilder has no namespaces
    and no import statements, every global object lives in one flat namespace, and symbol resolution
    is decided by that ordering. The cryptographic library this service replaces sits at position 19
    in it. There is no .slnx analogue and none is needed: this tree uses explicit namespaces and
    explicit project references instead. No ordering semantics are encoded below, no build order
    directive appears, and none may be added. The pairing of application before tests is for a human
    reader only; regenerating this file with the SDK would sort the two entries alphabetically and
    place the test project first, which is equally correct.

    The two PowerBuilder project objects were read for build intent only and are deliberately not
    translated, because neither is usable: they disagree with each other on library count and on
    vendor, one embeds developer workstation absolute paths together with a retired library name, and
    both name an image codec library that exists nowhere in the repository. This solution is a clean
    creation rather than a translation. No path below points at that read only tree, at any compiled
    PowerBuilder library, target or workspace, or at any native binary.

  RULES POSITION

    No user rules were provided for this project: the rules document contains exactly one line
    saying so. Nothing is invented or back filled from convention in their place. The binding
    constraints are the enterprise standard baseline together with the refactor's own named
    constraints, and every decision above cites the concern that drives it.
-->
<Solution>

  <Project Path="PowerFramework.Security/PowerFramework.Security.csproj" />
  <Project Path="PowerFramework.Security.Tests/PowerFramework.Security.Tests.csproj" />

</Solution>
